WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
Many movie stars don't have to pretend&nbsp;
to be badasses on the big screen because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.680
they're already badasses in real life! From&nbsp;
martial arts masters to decorated war heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.880
here are the most badass actors past and present.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.360
From his un-shaveable beard&nbsp;
to his unstoppable boot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.600
the real Chuck Norris is even more&nbsp;
badass than the legendary one.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.791
Born Carlos Ray Norris in 1940 in Ryan, Oklahoma,&nbsp;
Norris began training in the martial art form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.791 --> 00:00:32.560
Tang Soo Do and Judo while serving in the United&nbsp;
States Air Force in South Korea in the late 1960s.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.920
Norris won the world middleweight&nbsp;
karate championship six years in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.280
a row. He even founded his own style, a&nbsp;
Korean-American hybrid called Chun Kuk Do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.840
now known as "the Chuck Norris System."

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.920
Among his other real-life feats is&nbsp;
training badass Steve McQueen in karate,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.920
"I saw a movie called On Any Sunday. I said if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.160
there's any one actor I'd like to&nbsp;
meet, that's the man I'd like to meet."

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.160
"And so Steve became one of my private students."

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.840
Chuck Norris also had his own line of "Action&nbsp;
Jeans," and was the first Westerner to earn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:11.840
rank of eighth-degree black belt grandmaster in&nbsp;
Taekwondo. Norris hasn't lost a fight since 1968.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.000
The father of Western martial arts films,&nbsp;
Bruce Lee gave us some epic fight scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.280
But his big screen adventures weren't the result&nbsp;
of special effects — Lee was the real deal.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.800
And let's be honest: He probably would have kicked&nbsp;
Brad Pitt's ass in Once Upon A Time in Hollywood.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.920
While Bruce Lee was born in San Francisco in&nbsp;
1940, he became a child actor in Hong Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.680
where he also began training in&nbsp;
Kung Fu. Lee returned to the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.240
attending the University of Washington and&nbsp;
eventually opening a martial arts school.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.480
While Lee is best remembered for being&nbsp;
a pioneering Asian-American movie star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.400
who brought Kung Fu movies to American audiences,&nbsp;
he was also a once-in-a-generation martial artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.120
both onscreen and off. Bruce Lee developed&nbsp;
his own style called Jeet Kune Do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.440
or "Way of the Intercepting Fist".

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.200
Famously, he could even knock a man down with one-&nbsp;
and six-inch punches. Bruce Lee died in his early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.473
30s from cerebral edema.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.200
Ronda Rousey's filmography may&nbsp;
not have any Oscar contenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.280
but who needs to collect awards when&nbsp;
your nickname is "The Arm Collector"?

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.160
As a child, Rousey fell in love with Judo. In&nbsp;
2004, she competed in her first Olympics in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.080
Athens when she was just 17. Rousey returned four&nbsp;
years later to Beijing, winning the Bronze medal.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.680
She moved on to mixed martial arts,&nbsp;
becoming the female standard bearer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.120
for the Ultimate Fighting Championship,&nbsp;
winning the women's bantamweight belt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.720
and racking up a UFC record of 6-2,&nbsp;
and 12-2 for her total MMA career.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.320
A badass in the making, Rousey&nbsp;
brought mainstream attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.080
and millions of eyeballs to women's fighting,&nbsp;
and she made herself a major star in the process.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.640
In addition to her movie roles, Rousey is&nbsp;
also a professional wrestler with the WWE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.000
and she made history participating in the first&nbsp;
women's match main event at WrestleMania 35.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.840
Born Phillip Andre Rourke Jr. in Schenectady,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.520
New York, Mickey Rourke is not only a celebrated&nbsp;
actor, he's also got a devastating right hook.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.240
Rourke was throwing punches long before he&nbsp;
was reciting lines, racking up an impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:19.520
27-3 record, with 17 victories by knockout,&nbsp;
as an amateur boxer in the 1960s and 1970s.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.040
The fighter returned to the ring after&nbsp;
establishing himself as a thespian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.640
and then he returned again in 2014,&nbsp;
at age 62, fighting Elliot Seymour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.800
a 29-year old former California&nbsp;
state Golden Gloves champion.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.320
Rourke knocked Seymour down twice&nbsp;
before the referee stopped the fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.120
and awarded the win to Rourke. Seymour,&nbsp;
however, claims he took a dive.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.480
Whether or not the fight was fixed,&nbsp;
there's no denying that Rourke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.880
an Oscar-nominated actor for The&nbsp;
Wrestler, is badass on and off screen.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.360
Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson is one of the most&nbsp;
popular movie stars in Hollywood. Before he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.800
swinging from vines in the Jumanji series, revving&nbsp;
18-wheelers in The Fast and The Furious movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.600
or flexing his massive biceps on Instagram,&nbsp;
the Rock was doing all kinds of badass stuff.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.320
For one thing, Johnson was a successful pro&nbsp;
wrestler. He was also a college athlete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.400
playing defensive lineman for the National&nbsp;
Championship-winning Miami Hurricanes.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.480
Unfortunately for the Rock, he played the&nbsp;
same position as arguably the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.040
defensive linemen ever, Warren Sapp.&nbsp;
When Johnson asked Sapp what he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.480
doing in the defensive linemen&nbsp;
locker room, Sapp told Johnson,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.560
"I'm here for your job, b---h."

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.360
And that's exactly what he did. Warren&nbsp;
Sapp became a NFL Hall of Famer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.480
and Dwayne Johnson is, well, the Rock.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.000
"When this whole thing's over we're gonna find&nbsp;
a location and I'm gonna knock your teeth so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.280
far down your throat you're gonna stick a&nbsp;
toothbrush right up your ass to brush them."

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.040
Arnold Schwarzenegger practically invented the&nbsp;
modern action hero genre that defined the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.560
After all, he was a one-man army in&nbsp;
films like The Terminator, Predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.600
Total Recall, The Running Man, and Commando.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.880
But you don't take down thousands of bad guys&nbsp;
without breaking a sweat if you're not a badass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.800
Schwarzenegger built his larger-than-life legend&nbsp;
one curl, crunch, squat and press at a time.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.480
Arnold Alois Schwarzenegger&nbsp;
was born in Austria in 1947,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.560
but he dreamed of coming to America&nbsp;
and saw bodybuilding as his ticket.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.080
He racked up an incredible list of accomplishments&nbsp;
in bodybuilding, including seven Mr. Olympias.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.800
Schwarzenegger also served&nbsp;
as governor of California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.400
so there's that. For being the literal&nbsp;
definition of a self-made man and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.760
the most accomplished competitors in his sport's&nbsp;
history, Schwarzenegger is definitely a badass.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.880
Most movie stars would consider themselves&nbsp;
lucky to play one iconic big screen hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.920
Harrison Ford has played two — Indiana&nbsp;
Jones and Han Solo. Ford's heroic roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.160
are so memorable that he placed twice in the&nbsp;
top 15 on AFI's 100 greatest heroes list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.520
with the rugged wise-cracking&nbsp;
archaeologist ranking #2.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.960
"I hate snakes, Jock! I hate 'em!"

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.760
Throw in Rick Deckard from Blade Runner,&nbsp;
Dr. Richard Kimble from The Fugitive, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.200
Jack Ryan from the Tom Clancy&nbsp;
series, and Harrison Ford has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.720
plenty of unforgettable moments and characters&nbsp;
that put him in the badass hall of fame.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.760
However, Ford has proven the best way to be&nbsp;
a big screen hero is to be one in real life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.160
A trained airplane and helicopter pilot,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.080
"You ever have a flying dream? I have …”

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.680
Harrison Ford has come to the rescue&nbsp;
of several people in danger. In 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.680
he saved two hikers who got stranded at&nbsp;
the top of Table Mountain in Wyoming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.560
The very next year, he rescued a 13-year old&nbsp;
Boy Scout who got separated from his troop.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.160
Talk about a badass! Must be nice having&nbsp;
Indiana Jones come to your rescue!

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.520
“I told you! Don't call me Junior!"

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.600
Sir Christopher Lee is known for playing some&nbsp;
of cinema's most diabolical villains, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:06.640
Dracula, Saruman, and Francisco Scaramanga,&nbsp;
aka one of the most iconic Bond villains.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.840
While nobody was better at being bad in movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.200
Christopher Lee was a good guy in real life.&nbsp;
And he took on some of history's worst villains.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:21.440
Born in 1922, Lee served the Finnish in the&nbsp;
Winter War against the Soviet Union in 1939.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.680
He joined the Royal Air Force in 1940&nbsp;
and served in the Long Range Desert Group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.480
during World War II, where he fought behind&nbsp;
enemy lines and destroyed German aircraft.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.840
Lee kept quiet about his service&nbsp;
well into his later years, saying,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.840
"I was attached to the SAS from time to time, but&nbsp;
we are forbidden — former, present, or future — to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.680
discuss any specific operations. Let's just say&nbsp;
I was in Special Forces and leave it at that."

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:51.360
As if all this isn't badass enough, Lee&nbsp;
also set a Guinness World Record in 2007&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.840
for the most screen credits for a living&nbsp;
actor, 244 film and TV movies. On a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.320
list full of certifiable badasses,&nbsp;
Sir Christopher Lee may be king.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.160
Only a badass can go from inmate to&nbsp;
action star! A Los Angeles native,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.000
Danny Trejo became a criminal at an early&nbsp;
age, getting tossed into juvie after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.600
hitting a kid with a rock. And&nbsp;
he wasn't a one-time offender.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:24.880
"When you're on a CDC bus it's the only bus on&nbsp;
the freeway. Everybody knows you're a criminal."

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.440
He spent his teen years as the&nbsp;
getaway driver for his robber uncle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.000
and he even stabbed a sailor with a broken bottle.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.760
When Trejo was busted for selling&nbsp;
"heroin" to an undercover agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.280
though it was actually just sugar,&nbsp;
he was sent to prison. He became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.440
a celebrity in the slammer for his&nbsp;
boxing prowess, telling TMZ Sports,

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.760
"I was lightweight and welterweight&nbsp;
champion of every penitentiary I was in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.760
and I was in all of them."

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.400
Trejo did stints in San Quentin, Folsom,&nbsp;
and Soledad. But his background behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.920
bars isn't what makes him a&nbsp;
badass — it's his redemption.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.160
The actor turned his life around, earning his high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.400
school diploma in prison and serving as&nbsp;
a drug counselor after being released.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.960
For fighting and conquering his&nbsp;
demons, Trejo is a badass with merit!

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:15.040
Actor Audie Murphy is the most highly&nbsp;
decorated soldier in American military history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:21.120
Murphy was born in Texas in 1925, and he helped&nbsp;
raise his 11 brothers and sisters after his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.840
father abandoned the family, dropping out of&nbsp;
the fifth grade to earn money as a sharecropper.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.320
Following the bombing of&nbsp;
Pearl Harbor on December 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.640
1941, Audie Murphy falsified his age and&nbsp;
joined the Army at 17 years old in 1942.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.520
Murphy's most courageous act was mounting&nbsp;
an abandoned, burning tank destroyer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:46.320
with a single machine gun taking on six&nbsp;
German Panzer tanks and 250 infantrymen.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.920
Even more heroic:

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:52.560
Audie Murphy single-handedly stopped the&nbsp;
enemy advance, which allowed him to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.240
his men in driving the Germans from&nbsp;
Holtzwihr in 1945. He was that badass!

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.760
After the war's end, Murphy was discovered by&nbsp;
movie star James Cagney and began a successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:09.760
film career in Westerns. Murphy's high point as&nbsp;
an actor was playing himself in To Hell and Back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.800
though he wanted the story to reflect the&nbsp;
experience of all infantrymen, not just himself.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:15.920
Murphy said:

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.600
"I've always felt their story should&nbsp;
be told. I just play a part in it."

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.720
Before he was battling Klingons as Scotty,&nbsp;
the chief engineer of the USS Enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.240
James Doohan was fighting Nazis&nbsp;
in Europe during World War II.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.840
Despite his famous Scottish accent, Doohan was&nbsp;
actually Canadian, and when WWII broke out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.920
he joined the Royal Canadian Artillery&nbsp;
and was shipped to England in 1940.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.400
Doohan went years without seeing conflict,&nbsp;
training for an eventual invasion of Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:48.240
When D-Day finally arrived on June 6, 1944,&nbsp;
Doohan was a key part of the Normandy invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.200
single-handedly taking out two German&nbsp;
snipers without getting a single scratch!

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:58.160
However, Doohan didn't leave the war unscathed.&nbsp;
He took four shots in the legs and chest when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.960
Canadian soldier mistook him for a German and&nbsp;
opened fire. Remarkably, the chest bullet was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:07.520
stopped by a cigarette case. But one bullet shot&nbsp;
off Doohan's middle finger on his right hand.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:12.240
Following the war, Doohan had dreams of&nbsp;
pursuing a technical or scientific career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.800
but when he heard awful voice acting on&nbsp;
the radio, he said he could do better.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.040
And a Star Trek icon was born.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:27.840
"I may be captain by rank. But I never&nbsp;
wanted to be anything else but an engineer."

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.720
Mel Brooks is behind some of the funniest&nbsp;
comedies of all time — Blazing Saddles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.200
Young Frankenstein, The Producers,&nbsp;
Spaceballs, the list goes on.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.120
Despite his reputation as one of&nbsp;
America's most successful funnymen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.600
Brooks' military career is no laughing matter.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:49.520
Born Melvin Kaminsky in 1926 in Brooklyn, Brooks&nbsp;
was drafted into World War II. While overseas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:55.200
the man of comedy served as corporal in the 1104th&nbsp;
Engineer Combat Battalion, 78th Infantry Division.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.680
His job title was combat engineer and&nbsp;
one of his duties was defusing landmines.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.560
Yup! We'd say he's a bonafide badass!

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.880
Brooks also fought in the Battle of the&nbsp;
Bulge, a German counteroffensive that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.200
halted by Allied forces, effectively&nbsp;
stopping the Nazis in their tracks.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.640
Despite seeing the horrors of the&nbsp;
Nazis up close in World War II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.440
when Brooks came home, he figured the best way&nbsp;
to fight Nazi tyranny was by ridiculing them.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:23.760
Brooks' The Producers is perhaps best&nbsp;
known for the absurd musical number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.400
"Springtime for Hitler," which makes a&nbsp;
mockery of his old wartime adversaries.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:36.160
Don't let his folksy demeanor and all-around nice&nbsp;
guy status fool you — Jimmy Stewart was a badass.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.440
Born in Pennsylvania in 1908, James&nbsp;
Stewart studied architecture at Princeton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.360
and launched a successful acting&nbsp;
career on Broadway and in Hollywood.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.520
Despite being one of the brightest young&nbsp;
stars of the day — including his nomination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:55.280
for an Oscar in 1939 for Mr. Smith Goes to&nbsp;
Washington and winning an Oscar in 1941 for The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:01.040
Philadelphia Story — Stewart was drafted into&nbsp;
the Army in 1941 and didn't seek a deferment.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.080
In fact, when the Army said&nbsp;
he was too skinny to serve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.120
Stewart gained weight to make sure he'd qualify.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.120
Jimmy Stewart became a pilot in the Army&nbsp;
Air Force, ending his war service with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:16.320
20 combat missions and rising to the rank of&nbsp;
colonel. He remained in the United States Air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:22.080
Force Reserve and was eventually promoted&nbsp;
to the rank of brigadier general in 1959.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:27.760
His accolades include two Distinguished Flying&nbsp;
Crosses and the Presidential Medal of Freedom.

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:30.160
"So we are very proud to be here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:37.360
and we're going to do our best to be useful as&nbsp;
soldiers for the United States Army. Thank you."

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.320
Stewart was not only the nicest&nbsp;
guy in Hollywood history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.360
he was the highest-ranking&nbsp;
actor in military history.

